# Strapi URL: Build vs Runtime (Next.js 16)

## Why two URLs?

- **Runtime (e.g. Docker container):** Frontend and Strapi are on the same host. Use an internal URL (`STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL`, e.g. `http://host.docker.internal:8080/api`) to skip TLS and public Nginx.
- **Build (e.g. CI / Docker build stage):** The build runner is not in the same network as the running container. It cannot reach `host.docker.internal` or internal hostnames. Use a public URL (`STRAPI_BUILD_TIME_URL`, e.g. `https://api.infinitycolor.co/api`) so Server Component fetches during `next build` succeed.

Per **Next.js 16** docs ([environment variables](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/configuring/environment-variables), [version 16 upgrade](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/upgrading/version-16)): `process.env` is replaced at build time for static references. Reading env inside a **function** (`getStrapiServerUrl()`) ensures the value is read when the code runs—at build time in the build process, at request time in the server process—so build and runtime can use different env vars.

For reading env **at request time**, Next.js 16 requires calling `await connection()` from `next/server` at the start of the Server Component. Otherwise `process.env.STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL` is inlined at build (e.g. undefined in CI) and the runtime container env is never used. **Any page or layout that fetches from Strapi on the server and should use STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL must call `await connection()` before any code that calls `getStrapiServerUrl()`.** Example: PLP page calls `await connection()` then `getProducts()` so the internal URL is used in the container.

## Env vars

| Variable | When | Example |
|----------|------|--------|
| `STRAPI_BUILD_TIME_URL` | Set only in **build** (CI / Docker build). Leave unset in runtime container. | `https://api.infinitycolor.co/api` |
| `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL` | Set in **runtime** container. Optional at build if `STRAPI_BUILD_TIME_URL` is set. | `http://host.docker.internal:8080/api` |

**Priority:** `STRAPI_BUILD_TIME_URL` > `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL` > `API_BASE_URL`

## Code

- **Use** `getStrapiServerUrl()` from `@/constants/api` for all server-side Strapi fetches (PLP, homepage, settings, categories). Do not use `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL` or `STRAPI_SERVER_URL` for new server fetch code.
- **Defined in:** [frontend/src/constants/api.ts](frontend/src/constants/api.ts)
- **Used in:** PLP page, homepage service, public settings service, categories service.

## Nginx + host.docker.internal

- Nginx on the host can use `upstream` with `127.0.0.1:8080` (Strapi). The frontend container using `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8080/api` reaches the **host’s** port 8080, which is the same place Nginx’s `127.0.0.1:8080` points to. So the setup is compatible.
- On Linux Docker, if `host.docker.internal` is not available, add `extra_hosts: host.docker.internal:host-gateway` to the frontend service.

